<LegalLayout
  title="Privacy Policy - Balm"
  description="Balm's privacy policy: your Jira data stays on your device and in Atlassian. Balm does not use analytics, ads, or tracking."
  policy
>
  <p class="eyebrow">Private by default</p>
  <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
  <p class="effective">Last updated: 17 July 2026</p>

  <div class="summary">
    Balm is a native Jira client for people who want their work to stay where it already lives:
    on their device and in Atlassian. No analytics. No ads. No tracking SDKs. No third-party
    data brokers.
  </div>

  <h2>1. What Balm is</h2>
  <p>Balm is a native macOS and iOS client for Jira Cloud, developed by Kyle Scudder. You sign in with your own Atlassian account using OAuth 2.0. Balm then reads and writes Jira data on your behalf, from your device.</p>

  <h2>2. Information stored on your device</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Sign-in tokens are stored in the system Keychain on your device.</li>
    <li>Preferences, saved filters, and notification inbox data are cached locally so Balm can feel fast and work offline.</li>
    <li>System notifications, if you enable them, are generated by the app on your device.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>3. Information in Atlassian</h2>
  <p>Your Jira issues, projects, comments, attachments, users, and account permissions remain in Atlassian. Balm accesses them only through Atlassian's API using the access you grant.</p>
  <p>To keep read state consistent between your devices, Balm saves a small record of notification ids you have read as a hidden property on your own Atlassian user account. Other Jira users cannot see it. Revoking Balm's access disconnects the app from it.</p>

  <h2>4. The sign-in service</h2>
  <p>Balm uses one small service at <code>auth.balm.kylescudder.co.uk</code> because Atlassian requires a client secret when exchanging sign-in codes for tokens, and that secret cannot ship inside the app.</p>
  <p>During sign-in and token refresh, the service relays your authorization code or refresh token to Atlassian and returns the result to your device. It processes these values in transit only. It has no database, stores nothing, and does not log token contents.</p>

  <h2>5. What Balm does not collect</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>No analytics, telemetry, or crash reporting.</li>
    <li>No advertising identifiers or tracking across apps or websites.</li>
    <li>No sale of personal data.</li>
    <li>No hosted copy of your Jira issues, comments, attachments, or projects.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>6. Your controls</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Sign out in Balm's settings to remove tokens and cached app data from the device.</li>
    <li>Revoke Balm's access at <a href="https://id.atlassian.com/manage-profile/apps">id.atlassian.com Connected apps</a>.</li>
    <li>Delete the app to remove its local storage from your device.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>7. Changes</h2>
  <p>If this policy changes, the updated version will be posted here with a new date. Balm will not quietly change from a private, device-first app into an analytics or advertising product.</p>

  <h2>8. Contact</h2>
  <p>Questions or privacy requests: use the <a href="/support">support page</a>.</p>
</LegalLayout>
